# Secrets, approval, and honest chain work

## Secrets

Never expose, paste, log, or commit private keys, seed phrases, wallet secrets, `.env` files, or values like `DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY`. Use env vars or a secrets manager locally. If a secret appears in chat or a file, stop, do not repeat it, and keep it out of git.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — secret in source
const wallet = new Wallet("0xabc123...");

// ✅ GOOD — secret stays out of the repo
const wallet = new Wallet(vars.get("DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY"));
```

## Explicit approval only

Do not commit, push, deploy, delete files, change network settings, or run destructive/irreversible commands unless the user clearly asked for that action in this conversation. Preserve existing project history and deployment records (`projects/*/deployments/*`, READMEs, checklists). Do not rewrite, drop, or invent over those files.

## Verify chain facts

Before using a chain ID, RPC, explorer URL, or protocol contract address, confirm it from **current official docs** for that network. Do not reuse stale notes or guess.

```md
<!-- ❌ BAD -->
Chain ID: 1 (I think this is the testnet)

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
Chain ID, RPC, explorer URL, and protocol addresses: verified from current official docs
```

## Never fabricate onchain activity

Do not invent transaction hashes, deployment addresses, interactions, or builder footprints. If it did not happen on the target chain, do not record it as if it did. Prefer meaningful chain-relevant integrations over spam counters or fake activity.

Document every **real** deployment and interaction in the repo (network, chain ID, contract, address, deploy tx, date, verified writes/reads), same style as existing `projects/*/deployments/*.md` files.
